  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e4815f8682",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Reference and physical bias\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"figures/full_ldo_bias.png\" alt=\"Complete reference driven transistor bias circuit\" width=\"900\">\n",
    "\n",
    "NPROGRAM and RPROGRAM convert the external reference into a bias current. PSEED\n",
    "is diode connected. PCOPY mirrors its current into diode connected NBIAS, which\n",
    "sets BN. The gates of NTAIL, NLIM, and NBR connect to BN. No ideal current source\n",
    "sets these branch currents in the regulator.\n",
    "\n",
    "The bias equation is implicit because the gate to source voltage depends on the\n",
    "actual transistor current, drain voltage, and body bias:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "V_{\\mathrm{REF}}=V_{GS,\\mathrm{NPROGRAM}}\n",
    "  +I_{\\mathrm{PROGRAM}}R_{\\mathrm{PROGRAM}}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "With RPROGRAM = 33 kΩ, the nominal current is approximately 7.815 µA and RS is\n",
    "approximately 0.258 V. NTAIL is twice as wide as NBIAS, but its current is not\n",
    "exactly twice the bias current because the two drain voltages differ. The operating\n",
    "point table below evaluates the connected devices instead of assuming ideal mirrors.\n",
    "\n",
    "The same VREF drives NPROGRAM and the reference input of the error amplifier.\n",
    "Reference ripple can therefore affect both the error signal and the bias currents.\n",
    "The reference transfer experiment includes both paths.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "132c4a536d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Transistor error amplifier\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"figures/full_ldo_amplifier_input.png\" alt=\"Transistor differential input and local gain enhancement\" width=\"1000\">\n",
    "\n",
    "N1L and N1R compare VOUT and VREF. P3L and P3R are diode connected loads.\n",
    "NTAIL supplies the input pair current. When VOUT rises, the N1L current increases\n",
    "and AL falls. The N1R current falls and AR rises.\n",
    "\n",
    "P2L and P2R provide local positive feedback. QL follows AR through RSHAPEL and\n",
    "QR follows AL through RSHAPER. This raises differential gain. The capacitors\n",
    "from QL and QR to VAA progressively reduce that feedback at high frequency.\n",
    "The mechanism requires positive restoring current in each differential branch.\n",
    "It must not be sized at the edge of regeneration.\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"figures/full_ldo_amplifier_output.png\" alt=\"Amplifier output mirrors and compensation\" width=\"900\">\n",
    "\n",
    "P4L and P4R mirror the P3 currents with a width ratio of three. N5L and N5R form\n",
    "the NMOS output mirror. P4R sources current into EA and N5R sinks current from EA.\n",
    "When AL falls, P4L increases the N5L current and N5R removes more current from EA.\n",
    "When AR rises, P4R supplies less current. Both actions lower EA. The driver and\n",
    "NMOS pass stage have positive voltage gain, so VOUT falls toward VREF.\n",
    "\n",
    "For matched pairs below their internal mirror poles, the DC transconductance is\n",
    "approximately\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "g_{m,\\mathrm{EA}}(0)\\simeq\n",
    "\\frac{g_{m1}g_{m4}}\n",
    " {g_{m3}-g_{m2}+1/r_{o1}+1/r_{o2}+1/r_{o3}}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The transistor suffix denotes its numbered pair. This equation makes the gain\n",
    "enhancement and its sensitivity visible. The denominator must remain positive\n",
    "through the intended process and bias range.\n",
    "\n",
    "Let $R_E=r_{o,\\mathrm{P4R}}\\parallel r_{o,\\mathrm{N5R}}$. The series RCE and CEA\n",
    "branch connects EA to ground through EC. Its dominant impedance is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Z_E(s)\\simeq\n",
    "\\frac{R_E(1+sR_{\\mathrm{CE}}C_{\\mathrm{EA}})}\n",
    " {1+s(R_E+R_{\\mathrm{CE}})C_{\\mathrm{EA}}},\n",
    "\\qquad A(s)\\simeq g_{m,\\mathrm{EA}}(s)Z_E(s).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "RCE = 1 kΩ and CEA = 100 pF place the compensation zero near 1.59 MHz. With\n",
    "$R_E\\approx249$ kΩ, the pole is near 6.36 kHz. This helps explain why the supply\n",
    "transfer begins rising well before the precision loop crosses unity gain.\n",
    "The native transistor simulation retains the mirror poles and all terminal capacitances.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e8f4da33b8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Physical gate driver and pass array\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"figures/full_ldo_driver_pass.png\" alt=\"PMOS local gate feedback driver and NFET pass array\" width=\"1000\">\n",
    "\n",
    "PF senses EA at its gate and GATE at its source. Its drain feeds H. PUP has its\n",
    "gate at H and sources current from VAA into GATE. If GATE rises with EA fixed,\n",
    "PF delivers more current to H. H rises, PUP supplies less current, and GATE is\n",
    "restored. This is the local negative feedback loop of the super source follower.\n",
    "\n",
    "NLIM sinks current from H and establishes the standing PF current. It also provides\n",
    "the discharge path from GATE through PF. PUP can increase its sourcing current\n",
    "when H falls. The two directions are not symmetric, which matters during load steps.\n",
    "\n",
    "PBR and NBR establish BU. RH connects H to this finite impedance bias node.\n",
    "RH is not connected to an ideal AC ground. With VAA and BN held quiet,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Z_{BU}(0)\\simeq\n",
    "\\frac{1}{g_{m,\\mathrm{PBR}}+1/r_{o,\\mathrm{PBR}}+1/r_{o,\\mathrm{NBR}}}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "At nominal full load this is about 7.52 kΩ. The RH branch therefore contributes\n",
    "approximately RH + 7.52 kΩ, rather than RH alone, to the impedance at H.\n",
    "\n",
    "CR connects VIN to H. A rising VIN drives H upward, reduces PUP current, and\n",
    "drives GATE downward. This opposes the direct positive ripple transfer through\n",
    "NPASS. CR shapes a frequency dependent numerator. Its presence does not guarantee\n",
    "broadband rejection. The frequency sweep below measures the resulting band.\n",
    "CGATE = 100 pF restrains fast gate movement and changes the local poles.\n",
    "\n",
    "The 100 kΩ bleeder gives NPASS a defined minimum output current of approximately\n",
    "10 µA. It is included in the standing current and efficiency calculations. At zero\n",
    "external load, the regulator must still supply this current.\n",
    "\n",
    "At 50 mA the nominal gate voltage is approximately 1.649 V. The 1.8 V auxiliary\n",
    "rail supplies the gate headroom that the 1.2 V power rail cannot provide.\n",
    "NPASS has $g_m\\approx0.936$ S and $r_o\\approx12.22$ Ω at this bias.\n",
    "Its finite output resistance is already a substantial input ripple path.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b6ed251d2f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. Hand analysis of the connected loops\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"figures/full_ldo_driver_pass.png\" alt=\"Connected driver and pass stage for the current balance equations\" width=\"900\">\n",
    "\n",
    "The following equations retain the pass Cgs, Cgd, and Cds along with the intentional\n",
    "capacitors. They omit internal mirror and driver parasitic poles. The hand model\n",
    "explains current flow and dominant dynamics. The native transistor simulation\n",
    "retains those poles and terminal charges for the complete frequency sweep.\n",
    "\n",
    "With positive load current defined as current removed from VOUT, the pass source\n",
    "current balance is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\left[g_{mP}+\\frac{1}{r_{oP}}+\\frac{1}{R_{\\mathrm{BLEED}}}\n",
    " +s(C_{\\mathrm{OUT}}+C_{gsP}+C_{dsP})\\right]v_o\n",
    " =(g_{mP}+sC_{gsP})v_g\n",
    "  +\\left(\\frac{1}{r_{oP}}+sC_{dsP}\\right)v_{\\mathrm{IN}}-i_L.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Define $Z_O$ as the impedance seen at VOUT with GATE and VIN held at AC ground,\n",
    "including COUT and RBLEED. Its two voltage transfers are $P$ and $D$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Z_O=\\frac{1}{g_{mP}+1/r_{oP}+1/R_{\\mathrm{BLEED}}\n",
    "                    +s(C_{\\mathrm{OUT}}+C_{gsP}+C_{dsP})},\n",
    "\\quad P=(g_{mP}+sC_{gsP})Z_O,\n",
    "\\quad D=(1/r_{oP}+sC_{dsP})Z_O.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Thus $v_o=Pv_g+Dv_{\\mathrm{IN}}-Z_Oi_L$. The pass body is tied to its source,\n",
    "so the incremental body effect voltage is zero in this equation.\n",
    "\n",
    "With GATE, EA, VIN, VAA, and BN held quiet, the reduced H impedance is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Z_H=\\frac{1}{1/r_{o,\\mathrm{PF}}+1/r_{o,\\mathrm{NLIM}}\n",
    "             +1/(R_H+Z_{BU})+sC_R}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "For quiet supply and bias disturbances, the gate and H current balances are\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\left[g_{m,\\mathrm{PF}}+\\frac{1}{r_{o,\\mathrm{PF}}}\n",
    " +\\frac{1}{r_{o,\\mathrm{PUP}}}\n",
    " +s(C_{\\mathrm{GATE}}+C_{gsP}+C_{gdP})\\right]v_g\n",
    " +\\left(g_{m,\\mathrm{PUP}}-\\frac{1}{r_{o,\\mathrm{PF}}}\\right)v_H\n",
    " -sC_{gsP}v_o=g_{m,\\mathrm{PF}}v_E,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\frac{v_H}{Z_H}=\n",
    " \\left(g_{m,\\mathrm{PF}}+\\frac{1}{r_{o,\\mathrm{PF}}}\\right)v_g\n",
    " -g_{m,\\mathrm{PF}}v_E.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The PF source current leaves GATE and enters H. Its output resistance couples\n",
    "both nodes, which is why it appears in both equations.\n",
    "\n",
    "For the local loop break, open only the PUP transconductance control action.\n",
    "Retain its output resistance, the PF output resistance, and all passive connections.\n",
    "Hold EA and the independent disturbances quiet while allowing VOUT to move.\n",
    "Eliminating VOUT and H from the gate current balance gives the loaded gate\n",
    "impedance for this break:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Z_G=\\frac{1}{g_{m,\\mathrm{PF}}+1/r_{o,\\mathrm{PF}}+1/r_{o,\\mathrm{PUP}}\n",
    " +s(C_{\\mathrm{GATE}}+C_{gsP}+C_{gdP})-sC_{gsP}P\n",
    " -\\left(g_{m,\\mathrm{PF}}+1/r_{o,\\mathrm{PF}}\\right)Z_H/r_{o,\\mathrm{PF}}}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The Cgs term includes the bootstrap action of the moving output. The final term\n",
    "retains the reverse path through PF output resistance while PUP transconductance\n",
    "is open. Dropping either term changes the circuit at the loop break. Closing the\n",
    "PUP control gives the local return ratio and the resulting gate impedance:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "T_{\\mathrm{SSF}}=\n",
    "g_{m,\\mathrm{PUP}}\n",
    "\\left(g_{m,\\mathrm{PF}}+\\frac{1}{r_{o,\\mathrm{PF}}}\\right)Z_HZ_G,\n",
    "\\qquad Z_D=\\frac{Z_G}{1+T_{\\mathrm{SSF}}}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "At the nominal 50 mA operating point, these DC expressions give\n",
    "$Z_G\\approx1.516$ kΩ, $T_{\\mathrm{SSF}}\\approx6.280$, and\n",
    "$Z_D\\approx208.3$ Ω. These are analytical estimates from the operating point.\n",
    "$Z_G$ is a gate port impedance for the declared local break, not the impedance\n",
    "seen by the VIN source.\n",
    "\n",
    "The transfer from EA to VOUT, with this local loop closed, is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "P_E=PZ_Dg_{m,\\mathrm{PF}}\n",
    "\\left[1+\\left(g_{m,\\mathrm{PUP}}-\\frac{1}{r_{o,\\mathrm{PF}}}\\right)Z_H\\right].\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The VIN disturbance and output impedance with only the precision loop open are\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "D_{\\mathrm{IN}}=D+PZ_D\\left[sC_{gdP}+sC_{gsP}D\n",
    " -\\left(g_{m,\\mathrm{PUP}}-\\frac{1}{r_{o,\\mathrm{PF}}}\\right)Z_HsC_R\\right],\n",
    "\\qquad Z_{\\mathrm{OPEN}}=Z_O(1+PZ_DsC_{gsP}).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Here EA is held at its DC value with zero AC command. The physical local driver\n",
    "loop remains closed and VOUT is free to move. The output impedance is\n",
    "$-v_o/i_L$ with both supply rails and VREF quiet.\n",
    "\n",
    "The opposite sign of the CR term shows how it opposes the direct disturbance.\n",
    "The other terms show why one cancellation coefficient cannot remove every path\n",
    "over an unlimited frequency range.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "28f34d6072",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 7. Driver supply ripple and precision feedback\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"figures/full_ldo_driver_pass.png\" alt=\"Driver supply disturbance paths through PUP and the BU bias circuit\" width=\"900\">\n",
    "\n",
    "VAA enters through PUP, the BU bias branch, NLIM bias modulation, and the error\n",
    "amplifier. Let $B(s)=v_{BN}/v_{AA}$ with the physical bias generator connected.\n",
    "Below the BU pole, the current injected into H by a VAA disturbance is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\frac{i_{H,AA}}{v_{AA}}=\n",
    "\\frac{Z_{BU}}{R_H+Z_{BU}}\n",
    "\\left(g_{m,\\mathrm{PBR}}+\\frac{1}{r_{o,\\mathrm{PBR}}}\n",
    "                       -g_{m,\\mathrm{NBR}}B\\right)\n",
    " -g_{m,\\mathrm{NLIM}}B.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The corresponding driver and pass transfer is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "D_{AA}=PZ_D\\left[g_{m,\\mathrm{PUP}}+\\frac{1}{r_{o,\\mathrm{PUP}}}\n",
    " -\\left(g_{m,\\mathrm{PUP}}-\\frac{1}{r_{o,\\mathrm{PF}}}\\right)\n",
    "              Z_H\\frac{i_{H,AA}}{v_{AA}}\\right].\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Let $A_{AA}$ be the amplifier output response to VAA with its differential input\n",
    "quiet. Let $A_R$ and $D_R$ describe the additional shared reference paths through\n",
    "the bias circuit into the amplifier and driver. These are transfers of the physical\n",
    "transistor circuit. They are not behavioral controllers in the simulation.\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "v_E=A(v_{\\mathrm{REF}}-v_o)+A_{AA}v_{AA}+A_Rv_{\\mathrm{REF}},\n",
    "$$\n",
    "$$\n",
    "v_o=P_Ev_E+D_{\\mathrm{IN}}v_{\\mathrm{IN}}+D_{AA}v_{AA}\n",
    "                    +D_Rv_{\\mathrm{REF}}-Z_{\\mathrm{OPEN}}i_L.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Substitute the first equation into the second and collect the terms in $v_o$.\n",
    "The precision loop transfer is $T=A P_E$, with the local driver loop already\n",
    "closed. The resulting closed loop expressions are\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\frac{v_o}{v_{\\mathrm{IN}}}=\\frac{D_{\\mathrm{IN}}}{1+T},\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "\\frac{v_o}{v_{AA}}=\\frac{D_{AA}+P_EA_{AA}}{1+T},\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "Z_{\\mathrm{OUT}}=-\\frac{v_o}{i_L}=\\frac{Z_{\\mathrm{OPEN}}}{1+T},\n",
    "$$\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\frac{v_o}{v_{\\mathrm{REF}}}\n",
    " =\\frac{T+P_EA_R+D_R}{1+T}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The denominator is the feedback characteristic equation. The numerators describe\n",
    "different physical disturbance paths. Low output impedance and good supply rejection\n",
    "can therefore improve together without being the same property.\n",
    "\n",
    "The $Z_{\\mathrm{OUT}}$ experiment keeps both loops closed, holds VIN, VAA, and\n",
    "VREF quiet, and injects AC load current at VOUT. It is distinct from an input\n",
    "impedance experiment that perturbs VIN and measures current entering VIN.\n",
    "\n",
    "If both supply rails carry ripple from the same upstream source, their complex\n",
    "transfers must be summed with the actual rail amplitudes and phases before taking\n",
    "the magnitude. Adding the two dB curves would be incorrect.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4c2b27d8f9",
   "metadata": {},
