  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "26ce8e074e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 15. Test the ripple coupling mechanism\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"figures/full_ldo_driver_pass.png\" alt=\"VIN coupling capacitor CR and physical driver supply paths\" width=\"900\">\n",
    "\n",
    "Remove only CR and repeat the same connected circuit measurements. At 50 mA,\n",
    "CR improves the worst VIN transfer by approximately 1.59 dB but worsens the\n",
    "worst VAA transfer by approximately 11.70 dB.\n",
    "\n",
    "The two effects have the same physical connection. For a VIN disturbance, CR\n",
    "injects a signal into H that can oppose the pass drain disturbance. For a VAA\n",
    "disturbance, VIN is AC quiet, so CR instead terminates H capacitively. This changes\n",
    "the local loop and restricts the motion of H that helps suppress PUP supply ripple.\n",
    "The expressions for $Z_H$ and $D_{AA}$ include this dependency.\n",
    "\n",
    "This comparison shows why tuning only the VIN curve can give a poor overall design\n",
    "when the auxiliary supply is also noisy. The preferred CR value depends on the\n",
    "actual spectra at both rails and the required band, rather than on a single notch.\n"
   ]
  },
